elling and bruising\r\n* Pain on deep inspiration\r\n* Pain on coughing\r\n* Crepitus or palpable deformity\r\n* Reduced chest movement\r\n* Associated respiratory distress if there are intrathoracic injuries\r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Myocardial injury\r\n* Arrhythmias\r\n* Pulmonary contusion\r\n* Pneumothorax\r\n* Haemothorax\r\n* Rib fractures\r\n* Rare vascular injury\r\n* Respiratory complications\r\n\r\nBecause sternal fractures can indicate significant blunt chest trauma, associated cardiac and thoracic injuries must be assessed.\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\nConservative\r\n\r\nFor uncomplicated, stable fractures:\r\n\r\n* Analgesics\r\n* Ice application\r\n* Rest initially\r\n* Deep-breathing exercises\r\n* Coughing exercises\r\n* Chest physiotherapy\r\n* Early mobilization\r\n* Monitor for associated cardiopulmonary injury\r\n\r\nSurgical\r\n\r\nRarely required.\r\n\r\nIndications:\r\n\r\n* Severe displacement\r\n* Unstable fracture\r\n* Persistent severe pain\r\n* Non-union\r\n* Associated chest-wall instability\r\n* Failure of conservative treatment\r\n\r\nORIF with plates and screws may be performed when surgical stabilization is indicated.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. FRACTURE OF PELVIS\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nA pelvic fracture is a break in one or more bones forming the pelvic ring, including the ilium, ischium, pubis or sacrum. Pelvic fractures range from stable injuries to life-threatening unstable pelvic-ring injuries.\r\n\r\nMechanism of Injury\r\n\r\n1. High-energy trauma\r\n\r\n* RTA\r\n* Fall from height\r\n* Crush injury\r\n* Industrial accidents\r\n\r\n2. Low-energy trauma\r\n\r\n* Falls in elderly osteoporotic patients\r\n\r\nMechanisms of pelvic-ring injury\r\n\r\n* AP compression: pelvis opens like a book.\r\n* Lateral compression: inward compression of the pelvic ring.\r\n* Vertical shear: upward displacement of one side of the pelvis.\r\n* Combined mechanisms may occur.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\nLocal\r\n\r\n* Severe pelvic/hip pain\r\n* Inability to stand or walk\r\n* Pelvic tenderness\r\n* Swelling and bruising\r\n* Limb-length discrepancy\r\n* Pelvic deformity\r\n* Pain on movement\r\n\r\nAssociated features\r\n\r\n* Shock due to major blood loss\r\n* Abdominal injury\r\n* Urethral/bladder injury\r\n* Rectal injury\r\n* Nerve injury\r\n* Lower-limb vascular injury\r\n\r\nComplications\r\n\r\n* Massive haemorrhage and hypovolaemic shock\r\n* Pelvic organ injury\r\n* Bladder/urethral injury\r\n* Rectal injury\r\n* Neurovascular injury\r\n* DVT and pulmonary embolism\r\n* Infection\r\n* Malunion\r\n* Chronic pelvic pain\r\n* Gait abnormality\r\n* Sexual dysfunction\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nManagement of Pelvic Fracture\r\n\r\nA. Initial Management\r\n\r\nPelvic fractures are managed initially according to trauma principles.\r\n\r\n1. Airway and breathing assessment\r\n2. Circulation assessment\r\n3. Control external haemorrhage\r\n4. Avoid repeated manipulation of the pelvis.\r\n5. Apply a pelvic binder when an unstable pelvic-ring injury with suspected haemorrhage is present.\r\n6. Establish IV access.\r\n7. Fluid and blood-product resuscitation as required.\r\n8. Assess for abdominal, urinary, neurological and vascular injuries.\r\n9. Imaging with X-ray/CT according to the patient’s condition.\r\n\r\nA pelvic binder is used as an early temporary measure for suspected unstable pelvic-ring injury and should be positioned appropriately around the greater trochanters.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nB. Conservative Management\r\n\r\nUsed mainly for stable, minimally displaced pelvic fractures.\r\n\r\n* Bed rest initially when required\r\n* Analgesics\r\n* Pelvic support where appropriate\r\n* DVT prophylaxis\r\n* Early mobilization\r\n* Walking aids\r\n* Physiotherapy\r\n* Progressive weight bearing\r\n* Regular radiological follow-up\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nC. Surgical Management\r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Unstable pelvic-ring fracture\r\n* Significant displacement\r\n* Open pelvic fracture\r\n* Persistent haemodynamic instability\r\n* Failure of conservative management\r\n* Associated organ/vascular injury\r\n\r\nMethods\r\n\r\n* External fixation\r\n* Pelvic packing for uncontrolled pelvic haemorrhage in appropriate settings\r\n* Angioembolization for arterial bleeding\r\n* ORIF with plates and screws\r\n* Percutaneous screw fixation in selected fractures ////////////////INFLAMMATION AND REPAIR —-\r\n\r\nIntroduction to Soft-Tissue Healing\r\n\r\nDefinition of Inflammation\r\n\r\nInflammation is a protective response of living vascularized tissue to injury, infection or irritation, which removes the injurious stimulus and initiates the process of healing and repair.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Causes of Inflammation\r\n\r\n* Trauma and mechanical injury\r\n* Burns\r\n* Infection\r\n* Chemical irritants\r\n* Foreign bodies\r\n* Tissue necrosis\r\n* Ischaemia\r\n* Immune reactions\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Types of Inflammation\r\n\r\nA. Acute inflammation\r\n\r\n* Rapid onset\r\n* Usually lasts for a short duration\r\n* Predominantly involves neutrophils\r\n* Characterized by vascular changes, exudation of fluid and inflammatory cells.\r\n\r\nB. Chronic inflammation\r\n\r\n* Develops gradually or persists for a longer duration\r\n* Predominantly involves lymphocytes, macrophages and plasma cells\r\n* Associated with tissue destruction and fibrosis.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Cardinal Signs of Inflammation\r\n\r\n1. Rubor – redness\r\n2. Calor – heat\r\n3. Tumor – swelling\r\n4. Dolor – pain\r\n5. Functio laesa – loss of function\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Basic Process of Inflammation\r\n\r\nVascular response\r\n\r\n* Brief vasoconstriction\r\n* Vasodilation\r\n* Increased blood flow\r\n* Increased vascular permeability\r\n* Formation of inflammatory exudate\r\n* Stasis of blood flow\r\n\r\nCellular response\r\n\r\n* Margination of leukocytes\r\n* Rolling\r\n* Adhesion\r\n* Transmigration/diapedesis\r\n* Chemotaxis\r\n* Phagocytosis\r\n* Destruction/removal of microorganisms and damaged tissue\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Definition of Repair\r\n\r\nRepair is the restoration of tissue continuity following injury by replacement of damaged tissue with new tissue.\r\n\r\nRepair occurs through two major processes:\r\n\r\n1. Regeneration\r\n2. Scar formation\r\n\r\nRegeneration\r\n\r\nReplacement of damaged cells by cells of the same type, restoring normal tissue structure and function.\r\n\r\nScar formation\r\n\r\nReplacement of damaged tissue by connective tissue, resulting in fibrosis and scar formation.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Soft-Tissue Healing\r\n\r\nSoft-tissue healing is the process by which injured skin, muscle, tendon, ligament and other soft tissues restore their structural integrity and function following injury.\r\n\r\nHealing occurs through overlapping phases.\r\n\r\nPhase 1: Haemostasis\r\n\r\nImmediately after injury\r\n\r\n* Vasoconstriction\r\n* Platelet aggregation\r\n* Formation of blood clot\r\n* Fibrin clot formation\r\n* Provides temporary wound stability\r\n* Releases growth factors that initiate healing\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPhase 2: Inflammatory Phase\r\n\r\nApproximately first few days\r\n\r\n* Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability\r\n* Neutrophils migrate to the wound\r\n* Macrophages subsequently predominate\r\n* Removal of bacteria, dead cells and debris\r\n* Release of cytokines and growth factors\r\n* Prepares the wound for tissue formation\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPhase 3: Proliferative Phase\r\n\r\nUsually begins within a few days and continues for several weeks.\r\n\r\nMain events\r\n\r\n* Fibroblast proliferation\r\n* Collagen synthesis\r\n* Formation of granulation tissue\r\n* Angiogenesis\r\n* Re-epithelialization\r\n* Wound contraction\r\n\r\nInitially, the wound contains predominantly type III collagen.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nPhase 4: Remodeling/Maturation\r\n\r\nBegins after the proliferative phase and may continue for months to years.\r\n\r\n* Collagen is reorganized\r\n* Type III collagen is gradually replaced by stronger type I collagen\r\n* Collagen cross-linking increases\r\n* Vascularity decreases\r\n* Scar becomes paler and flatter\r\n* Tensile strength progressively increases but does not normally return completely to that of uninjured tissue.\r\n\r\nThe inflammatory, proliferative and remodeling phases overlap rather than occurring as completely separate stages.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Factors Affecting Soft-Tissue Healing\r\n\r\nLocal factors\r\n\r\n* Poor blood supply\r\n* Infection\r\n* Tissue damage\r\n* Foreign body\r\n* Excessive tension\r\n* Repeated trauma\r\n* Haematoma\r\n* Excessive movement\r\n\r\nSystemic factors\r\n\r\n* Age\r\n* Poor nutrition\r\n* Diabetes mellitus\r\n* Smoking\r\n* Anaemia\r\n* Immunosuppression\r\n* Certain medications, particularly corticosteroids\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Types of Wound Healing\r\n\r\nPrimary intention\r\n\r\n* Wound edges are closely approximated.\r\n* Minimal tissue loss.\r\n* Less granulation tissue.\r\n* Small scar.\r\n* Example: clean surgical incision.\r\n\r\nSecondary intention\r\n\r\n* Wound edges cannot be approximated.\r\n* Greater tissue loss.\r\n* Extensive granulation tissue.\r\n* More wound contraction.\r\n* Larger scar.\r\n\r\nTertiary intention\r\n\r\n* Wound is initially left open because of contamination/infection and is closed later after the wound is adequately clean./////////////1. Sprain\r\n\r\nDefinition: Injury to a ligament caused by excessive stretching or tearing, usually due to sudden twisting or abnormal joint movement.\r\n\r\nFeatures: Pain, swelling, tenderness and joint instability.\r\n\r\n2. Strain\r\n\r\nDefinition: Injury to a muscle or tendon caused by overstretching or excessive force.\r\n\r\nFeatures: Pain, muscle spasm, weakness and swelling.\r\n\r\n3. Contusion\r\n\r\nDefinition: Injury to soft tissue caused by a direct blunt blow, producing damage to underlying tissues without breaking the skin. \r\n/////2. Meniscal Injuries of Knee\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\n* Twisting of a weight-bearing knee\r\n* Sudden change of direction\r\n* Deep squatting\r\n* Direct trauma\r\n* Degenerative injury in older individuals\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Joint-line pain and tenderness\r\n* Swelling, often developing after activity\r\n* Locking or catching\r\n* Clicking\r\n* Restricted knee movement\r\n* Pain on squatting\r\n* Positive McMurray test and/or Apley compression test\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Cruciate Ligament Injuries of Knee\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\nACL injury\r\n\r\n* Sudden deceleration\r\n* Pivoting/change of direction\r\n* Hyperextension\r\n* Valgus force with rotation\r\n\r\nPCL injury\r\n\r\n* Dashboard injury\r\n* Fall onto a flexed knee\r\n* Hyperflexion\r\n* Posteriorly directed force on the tibia\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Pain and swelling\r\n* Acute haemarthrosis, particularly with ACL injury\r\n* Feeling of instability or giving way\r\n* Restricted ROM\r\n* ACL: Positive Lachman and anterior drawer tests\r\n* PCL: Positive posterior drawer test and posterior sag sign\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Medial and Lateral Collateral Ligament Injuries\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\nMCL\r\n\r\n* Valgus stress applied to the knee\r\n* Commonly associated with contact sports\r\n\r\nLCL\r\n\r\n* Varus stress applied to the knee\r\n* May occur with high-energy trauma\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Pain and tenderness over the injured ligament\r\n* Local swelling\r\n* Pain on stress testing\r\n* Medial instability with valgus stress in MCL injury\r\n* Lateral instability with varus stress in LCL injury\r\n* Difficulty in weight bearing\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Lateral Ligament Injury of Ankle\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\nMost commonly caused by inversion injury of a plantar-flexed ankle.\r\n\r\nCommonly injured ligaments:\r\n\r\n* Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)\r\n* Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)\r\n* Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Pain around lateral ankle\r\n* Swelling\r\n* Bruising\r\n* Tenderness over lateral ligaments\r\n* Difficulty in weight bearing\r\n* Reduced ankle ROM\r\n* Instability in severe injury\r\n* Positive anterior drawer/talar tilt tests when appropriate\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Wrist Sprain\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\n* Fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH)\r\n* Sudden hyperextension or hyperflexion\r\n* Twisting injury\r\n* Direct trauma\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\n* Wrist pain\r\n* Swelling\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Painful and restricted wrist movement\r\n* Reduced grip strength\r\n* Bruising\r\n* Pain during loading of the wrist/////Strains, Contusions and Tendon Ruptures—-\r\n\r\n1. Strains\r\n\r\nDefinition: A strain is an injury involving a muscle or musculotendinous unit, caused by excessive stretching or forceful contraction.\r\n\r\nA. Quadriceps strain\r\n\r\nMechanism: Sudden forceful contraction, sprinting, kicking or jumping.\r\nClinical features:\r\n\r\n* Anterior thigh pain\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Swelling/bruising\r\n* Pain on resisted knee extension\r\n* Weakness of quadriceps\r\n\r\nB. Hamstring strain\r\n\r\nMechanism: Sudden sprinting, acceleration or excessive hip flexion with knee extension.\r\nClinical features:\r\n\r\n* Posterior thigh pain\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Bruising/swelling\r\n* Pain on resisted knee flexion\r\n* Reduced running ability\r\n\r\nC. Calf strain\r\n\r\nMechanism: Sudden push-off, jumping or running.\r\nClinical features:\r\n\r\n* Calf pain\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Swelling\r\n* Pain on resisted plantar flexion\r\n* Difficulty walking\r\n\r\nD. Biceps strain\r\n\r\nMechanism: Sudden forceful elbow flexion or lifting.\r\nClinical features:\r\n\r\n* Anterior arm pain\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Pain on resisted elbow flexion/supination\r\n* Weakness\r\n\r\nE. Triceps strain\r\n\r\nMechanism: Forceful elbow extension or throwing activity.\r\nClinical features:\r\n\r\n* Posterior arm pain\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Pain on resisted elbow extension\r\n* Weakness and swelling\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Contusions\r\n\r\nDefinition: A contusion is a blunt-trauma injury causing damage to muscle and small blood vessels without disruption of the skin.\r\n\r\nA. Quadriceps contusion\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by direct blow to the thigh.\r\n* Pain, swelling and bruising\r\n* Reduced knee flexion\r\n* Muscle stiffness\r\n* Severe cases may develop myositis ossificans\r\n\r\nB. Gluteal contusion\r\n\r\n* Direct fall or blow to buttock.\r\n* Pain and tenderness\r\n* Swelling and bruising\r\n* Pain while sitting or walking\r\n\r\nC. Calf contusion\r\n\r\n* Direct blow to calf.\r\n* Pain and swelling\r\n* Bruising\r\n* Tenderness\r\n* Painful walking and plantar flexion\r\n\r\nD. Deltoid contusion\r\n\r\n* Direct blow to shoulder.\r\n* Shoulder pain and tenderness\r\n* Swelling/bruising\r\n* Painful shoulder movement\r\n* Reduced abduction\r\n\r\nGeneral management: Protection/rest initially, ice, compression and elevation where appropriate, followed by gradual ROM and strengthening.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Tendon Ruptures\r\n\r\nA. Achilles Tendon Rupture\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\n* Sudden forceful plantar flexion\r\n* Jumping or sprinting\r\n* Sudden eccentric loading of calf muscles\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Sudden severe pain in posterior ankle\r\n* Feeling of being kicked in the calf\r\n* Difficulty walking\r\n* Weak/absent plantar flexion\r\n* Palpable gap above calcaneus\r\n* Positive Thompson test\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Immobilization in plantar flexion for selected patients\r\n* Functional rehabilitation\r\n* Surgical tendon repair in selected active patients, complete ruptures and appropriate cases.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nB. Rotator Cuff Tendon Rupture\r\n\r\nMechanism\r\n\r\n* Fall on an outstretched arm\r\n* Forceful shoulder movement\r\n* Repetitive overhead activity\r\n* Degenerative tendon changes\r\n\r\nClinical features\r\n\r\n* Shoulder pain\r\n* Painful arc\r\n* Weakness of shoulder elevation\r\n* Difficulty with overhead activities\r\n* Night pain\r\n* Positive rotator-cuff tests\r\n\r\nManagement\r\n\r\n* Rest/activity modification\r\n* Analgesics\r\n* Physiotherapy: ROM and progressive rotator-cuff/scapular strengthening\r\n* Surgical repair for selected acute traumatic tears, significant weakness or failure of conservative treatment.//////////////////AMPUTATIONS\r\n\r\nDefinition and Levels of Amputation\r\n—\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nAmputation is the partial or complete removal of a limb or part of a limb through a surgical procedure or as a result of trauma.\r\n\r\nThe main aims of amputation are to remove non-viable or diseased tissue, control infection or pain, and create a functional residual limb suitable for rehabilitation and prosthetic fitting.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Levels of Upper-Limb Amputation\r\n\r\nA. Finger and Hand\r\n\r\n1. Distal phalanx amputation\r\n2. Middle phalanx amputation\r\n3. Proximal phalanx amputation\r\n4. Metacarpal amputation\r\n5. Ray amputation – removal of a digit with its corresponding metacarpal.\r\n6. Transmetacarpal amputation – through the metacarpals.\r\n\r\nB. Wrist and Forearm\r\n\r\n7. Wrist disarticulation – separation through the wrist joint.\r\n8. Transradial amputation – through the forearm, below the elbow.\r\n\r\nC. Elbow and Arm\r\n\r\n9. Elbow disarticulation – through the elbow joint.\r\n10. Transhumeral amputation – through the arm, above the elbow.\r\n\r\nD. Shoulder\r\n\r\n11. Shoulder disarticulation – removal of the upper limb through the shoulder joint.\r\n12. Forequarter amputation – removal of the upper limb together with part of the shoulder girdle.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Levels of Lower-Limb Amputation\r\n\r\nA. Foot and Ankle\r\n\r\n1. Toe amputation\r\n2. Ray amputation\r\n3. Transmetatarsal amputation\r\n4. Lisfranc amputation – through the tarsometatarsal joints.\r\n5. Chopart amputation – through the transverse tarsal joint.\r\n6. Syme’s amputation – ankle disarticulation with preservation of the heel pad.\r\n\r\nB. Leg\r\n\r\n7. Transtibial amputation – through the tibia and fibula below the knee.\r\n\r\nC. Knee\r\n\r\n8. Knee disarticulation – amputation through the knee joint.\r\n\r\nD. Thigh and Hip\r\n\r\n9. Transfemoral amputation – through the femur above the knee.\r\n10. Hip disarticulation – removal of the limb through the hip joint.\r\n11. Hemipelvectomy – removal of the lower limb with part of the pelvis.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Indications for Amputation\r\n\r\n* Severe traumatic injury\r\n* Peripheral vascular disease with irreversible tissue loss\r\n* Uncontrolled infection/gangrene\r\n* Malignant tumour\r\n* Severe congenital deformity\r\n* Non-functional painful limb\r\n* Severe burns or crush injury\r\n* Irreversible ischemia\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Principles of Selecting the Level\r\n\r\nThe level should be selected to:\r\n\r\n* Remove all non-viable or diseased tissue.\r\n* Preserve maximum useful limb length.\r\n* Preserve viable skin and soft-tissue coverage.\r\n* Maintain adequate blood supply.\r\n* Preserve important joints whenever possible.\r\n* Produce a stable, painless and functional residual limb.\r\n* Allow effective prosthetic fitting./////////////////////Traumatic Spinal Cord Injuries—\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nTraumatic spinal cord injury (SCI) is an acute injury to the spinal cord caused by an external mechanical force, resulting in temporary or permanent impairment of motor, sensory and autonomic functions below the level of injury. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nCauses\r\n\r\n* Road-traffic accidents\r\n* Falls from height\r\n* Sports injuries, especially diving\r\n* Direct blow to the spine\r\n* Gunshot or penetrating injuries\r\n* Crush injuries\r\n* Hyperflexion or hyperextension injuries\r\n* Fracture-dislocation of the vertebral column\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\nClinical Features\r\n\r\nClinical presentation depends mainly on the level, severity and completeness of spinal cord injury.\r\n\r\n1. Motor disturbances\r\n\r\n* Muscle weakness or paralysis below the level of injury\r\n* Paraplegia: paralysis of both lower limbs\r\n* Quadriplegia/tetraplegia: paralysis of all four limbs following cervical cord injury\r\n* Loss of voluntary movements\r\n* Muscle weakness may be flaccid initially because of spinal shock\r\n* Later, spasticity and hyperreflexia may develop in upper motor neuron lesions\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Sensory disturbances\r\n\r\n* Loss or reduction of sensation below the level of injury\r\n* Loss of:\r\n    * Pain\r\n    * Temperature\r\n    * Touch\r\n    * Pressure\r\n    * Vibration\r\n    * Proprioception\r\n* Paraesthesia or numbness\r\n* A clearly defined sensory level may be present.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Reflex changes\r\n\r\nSpinal shock\r\n\r\nImmediately after a significant acute SCI:\r\n\r\n* Flaccid paralysis\r\n* Loss of deep tendon reflexes\r\n* Loss of superficial reflexes\r\n* Absent bulbocavernosus reflex may occur\r\n* Areflexia below the level of injury\r\n\r\nAs spinal shock resolves, reflexes may return and become exaggerated in upper motor neuron injuries. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Autonomic disturbances\r\n\r\nBladder dysfunction\r\n\r\n* Urinary retention\r\n* Urinary incontinence\r\n* Neurogenic bladder\r\n\r\nBowel dysfunction\r\n\r\n* Constipation\r\n* Faecal incontinence\r\n* Neurogenic bowel\r\n\r\nSexual dysfunction\r\n\r\n* Erectile/ejaculatory dysfunction\r\n* Altered sexual sensation\r\n\r\nOther autonomic abnormalities may include impaired sweating and temperature regulation. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Respiratory Features\r\n\r\nRespiratory impairment depends on the level of injury.\r\n\r\n* Difficulty in deep inspiration\r\n* Weak cough\r\n* Retained respiratory secretions\r\n* Reduced vital capacity\r\n* Respiratory muscle paralysis\r\n* Respiratory failure in high cervical injuries\r\n\r\nInjury involving C3–C5 can compromise diaphragmatic function because the phrenic nerve arises from these cervical segments. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Cardiovascular Features\r\n\r\nParticularly with cervical and high thoracic injuries:\r\n\r\n* Hypotension\r\n* Bradycardia\r\n* Peripheral vasodilation\r\n* Neurogenic shock\r\n* Cardiac rhythm disturbances\r\n\r\nNeurogenic shock results from loss of sympathetic outflow and is particularly associated with injuries above approximately T6. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Clinical Patterns of Incomplete SCI\r\n\r\nA. Central Cord Syndrome\r\n\r\n* Usually follows cervical hyperextension injury.\r\n* Upper limbs are affected more than lower limbs.\r\n* Hand weakness may be prominent.\r\n* Sensory disturbance may occur.\r\n\r\nB. Brown-Séquard Syndrome\r\n\r\nDue to hemisection of the spinal cord:\r\n\r\n* Ipsilateral motor weakness\r\n* Ipsilateral loss of proprioception/vibration\r\n* Contralateral loss of pain and temperature\r\n\r\nC. Anterior Cord Syndrome\r\n\r\n* Motor paralysis below the lesion\r\n* Loss of pain and temperature\r\n* Relative preservation of dorsal-column functions such as vibration and proprioception. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Complete and Incomplete Injury\r\n\r\nComplete SCI\r\n\r\n* No sensory or motor function is preserved in the sacral segments S4–S5.\r\n* No sacral sparing.\r\n* Classified as AIS/ASIA Grade A. \r\n\r\nIncomplete SCI\r\n\r\n* Some sensory and/or motor function is preserved below the neurological level, including sacral function.\r\n* Classified as AIS B–D depending on the amount of preserved function.\r\n* AIS E: normal motor and sensory examination. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Clinical Examination\r\n\r\nAssessment should include:\r\n\r\n* Level of neurological injury\r\n* Motor power\r\n* Sensory examination\r\n* Deep tendon reflexes\r\n* Anal sensation and voluntary anal contraction\r\n* Muscle tone\r\n* Respiratory function\r\n* Bladder and bowel function\r\n* Autonomic function\r\n\r\nThe International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury (ISNCSCI/ASIA examination) is used to determine the neurological level and severity of injury. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n10. Important Complications\r\n\r\n* Respiratory failure\r\n* Neurogenic shock\r\n* Spinal shock\r\n* Pressure sores\r\n* Deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism\r\n* Urinary tract infection\r\n* Neurogenic bladder and bowel\r\n* Contractures\r\n* Spasticity\r\n* Autonomic dysreflexia\r\n* Chronic pain\r\n* Permanent paralysis/////////////////\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-26T17:00:24+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
